          body:
            "[[multiple-sclerosis|Multiple sclerosis]] (MS) is a chronic immune-mediated disease of the central nervous system: the brain, spinal cord, and optic nerves. Inflammation causes patches of [[demyelination|demyelination]] (plaques) with variable axonal loss. It usually begins in young adults and affects women more often than men.\n\nThe first episode is called a [[clinically-isolated-syndrome|clinically isolated syndrome]] (CIS): a first attack typical of demyelination, lasting at least 24 hours, with symptoms that build over hours to days, plateau, and then improve over weeks to months. Three presentations are typical.\n\n1. [[optic-neuritis|Optic neuritis]]: one eye, reduced acuity, washed-out colors, and pain that is worse on eye movement. There is a [[rapd|relative afferent pupillary defect]] on the affected side. The optic disc usually looks normal, because the inflammation sits behind it. Vision starts to recover within weeks in most people.\n2. Partial [[transverse-myelitis|myelitis]]: numbness or tingling that is often asymmetric, a band around the trunk, an electric feeling down the spine on bending the neck ([[lhermitte-sign|Lhermitte sign]]), bladder urgency, and [[umn|upper motor neuron]] signs. The word partial matters: the cord is only partly involved, and complete paralysis below a level is unusual.\n3. Brainstem or cerebellar syndromes: [[ino|internuclear ophthalmoplegia]] (bilateral INO in a young adult is classic for MS), double vision from a sixth nerve palsy, facial numbness, [[vertigo|vertigo]], or [[ataxia|ataxia]].\n\nHemispheric attacks also occur, usually as sensory symptoms or mild weakness on one side.",
