          body:
            "A [[ms-relapse|relapse]] (attack) is new or worsening neurological symptoms typical of demyelination, lasting at least 24 hours, in the absence of fever or infection. Symptoms that flare with heat, fever, or infection and settle when the trigger passes are a [[pseudo-relapse|pseudo-relapse]], not new inflammation.\n\nCourses are described as:\n\n- [[relapsing-remitting-ms|Relapsing-remitting MS]]: attacks with full or partial recovery and stable periods between. Most people start this way.\n- Secondary progressive MS: after a relapsing phase, gradual worsening of disability independent of relapses.\n- Primary progressive MS: gradual worsening from onset without preceding relapses, often as a slowly progressive spastic paraparesis. It tends to start later and affects men and women more evenly.\n\nEach course can be further described as active (relapses or new MRI lesions) or not active, and progressing or not. Disability accumulates in two ways: incomplete recovery from relapses, and slow progression that continues even without relapses.",
